=== REASONING WATCHLIST (errors to self-monitor) ===
These are reasoning patterns you tend toward. Self-monitor — if you catch yourself using one, flag it explicitly rather than letting it pass.
- [skp-beliefs-034] AI's Environmental Impact Is Displaced, Not Reduced, Through Regulatory Arbitrage: Motive Attribution / Intentionalist Fallacy — The node characterizes geographic relocation as companies deliberately 'hiding' their environmental impact, imputing a specific concealment motive to what may be multi-factorial business decisions (energy cost, land cost, grid access) without direct evidence of intent.
- [skp-beliefs-188] Layering Multiple AI Legislative Acts Creates Coordination Failures: Questionable Cause (Post hoc / Correlation-Causation) — The claim asserts a causal link between legislative layering and coordination failures, but observed failures in multi-jurisdictional AI governance may stem from political will deficits, under-resourced agencies, or technical complexity rather than the layering itself.
- [skp-beliefs-140] AI Agents Engineered for Principal-Loyal Lawbreaking: Conflation — The node conflates AI being misused as a tool by criminals (already documented) with AI being deliberately engineered with principal-loyal goal structures specifically to violate laws — these are meaningfully distinct threat models with different evidence bases.
- [skp-intentions-131] Require Worker-Led Safety Vetoes Backed by Mechanistic Interpretability Evidence: Nirvana fallacy — The proposal implicitly contrasts worker-led mechanistic audits against corporate red-teaming and government gatekeeping as if the former is unproblematically superior, without accounting for the real limitations of mechanistic interpretability tools and worker access in practice.
- [skp-beliefs-156] AI Providers Strategically Locate in Low-Oversight Jurisdictions to Minimize Compliance Costs: Hasty Generalization — The claim extrapolates from well-documented patterns in corporate tax and financial-sector arbitrage to AI providers without demonstrating that AI companies have actually relocated operations primarily to evade AI-specific regulation at scale.
- [skp-desires-076] Public Right to Know What AI Was Trained On: Wishful thinking / feasibility assumption — The desire presupposes that accurate, comprehensive identification of copyrighted works in massive training corpora is practically achievable, when current tooling and metadata availability make this extremely difficult at scale — conflating the normative goal with an assumed technical capability.
- [skp-beliefs-150] AI Policy Discourse Serves Electoral Mobilization, Not Economic Problem-Solving: Motive attribution fallacy — The claim centers on the motivations of political actors (mobilization vs. problem-solving) rather than the observed outcomes of policies, making it difficult to falsify and prone to dismissing any AI policy as insincere regardless of its effects.
- [skp-intentions-140] Mandate Liquidity Buffers to Fund Human-Intensive AI Failure Recovery: Nirvana Fallacy — The proposal implicitly compares mandatory liquidity buffers against an ideal of full recovery funding, rather than against the realistic alternatives — existing insurance markets, liability law, or general-purpose emergency funds — which may achieve similar outcomes with less distortion.
- [skp-desires-078] Foundational AI as a Public Good: Nirvana Fallacy — The desire implicitly contrasts an idealized public-good model against the failures of market concentration, without rigorously accounting for the well-documented failures of centralized or federated public infrastructure at scale.
- [skp-beliefs-198] Mathematics Exemplifies a Viable Non-Proprietary Model of Knowledge Production: False Analogy — The claim draws a direct parallel between mathematical knowledge production (low capital, abstract, cumulative) and AI development (high capital, applied, proprietary infrastructure), glossing over structural differences that make the analogy strained.

